## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Confirm comprehension of peer contributions during discussions.<br>concept: Use diagrams to explain concepts to peers.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can confirm understanding of peer contributions by writing keywords and showing visuals (e.g., write 'bring materials' and point to a list of supplies).<br>Can use diagrams to explain weather patterns to peers (e.g., draw a sun and clouds to show sunny and rainy weather).<br>Can summarize weather-related concepts for classmates by combining words and diagrams (e.g., write 'water cycle' next to a simple diagram).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of simple weather symbols (e.g., a sun, a cloud with rain, a cloud with wind). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what each symbol means and how it relates to weather in Panama. Teacher asks volunteers to *explain* one symbol to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to confirm understanding using a weather example. "I predict it will be <b>cloudy</b> tomorrow." (Teacher draws a cloud on the board). "Do you understand 'cloudy'?" (Teacher gestures for nods). "So, cloudy means many clouds, not much sun." (Teacher points to the cloud drawing again). Teacher also models segmenting 'thermostat' into 'ther-mo-stat' while showing the word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "It will be a windy day." (Teacher draws wind lines). "Does 'windy' mean a lot of breeze or no breeze?" (Expected answer: "A lot of breeze.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "The forecast expects rain." "Does 'expect' mean it will definitely happen or maybe happen?" (Expected answer: "Maybe happen.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated weather report chart for "Tomorrow's Weather in Our City." The chart includes short phrases like: "Morning: sunny. Temperature: 28°C. Afternoon: partly cloudy. Evening: chance of rain. You should bring an umbrella." It also has small icons (sun, cloud, rain cloud) and an arrow showing a slight temperature decrease.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe weather conditions: 'It will be sunny tomorrow.' Ask about the forecast: 'What's the temperature tomorrow?' Use prepositions of place: 'The rain is in the east.' Engage in peer discussions on weather-related topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What will the weather mostly be like tomorrow: hot and dry, or changing with some rain?" (Students point to the correct option or say it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It will be cloudy all day tomorrow." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should bring an umbrella in the evening." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Confirming understanding and using diagrams to explain concepts. Grammar: Future simple tense ('It will be sunny'), Prepositions of place ('The storm is over the lake'), Modal verbs ('You should bring'). Vocabulary: `weather`, `forecast`, `cloudy`, `rainy`, `windy`, `temperature`, `predict`, `will be`, `look like`, `bring`. Practice intonation in predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Keyword & Visual Match:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences about future weather and blank spaces for drawings. Example: "Tomorrow, it <b>will be sunny</b>." (Student draws a sun). "The <b>temperature will decrease</b>." (Student draws a thermometer with an arrow pointing down). Students complete the drawings and then, in pairs, *explain* their drawing to their partner, saying the sentence and pointing to their visual.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Diagram-Based Predictions & Confirmation:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A receives a card with a weather prediction (e.g., "It will be rainy over the mountains tomorrow. You should bring a jacket."). Partner A reads the prediction. Partner B *draws* a simple diagram representing the prediction (mountains, rain, jacket icon) and then *confirms* by asking, "So, it will be rainy over the mountains? And I should bring a jacket?" Partner A confirms or corrects. Students practice intonation, emphasizing the last word of the sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Summarize & Share:</b> The teacher shows a short paragraph about the forecast for a different region (e.g., "In the west, it will be very windy, with a chance of a storm in the evening."). Students work individually to *summarize* the main points by writing 3-4 keywords and drawing one small diagram. Then, in groups of three, each student *explains* their summary to the others using their keywords and drawing, and the other group members *confirm* their understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in their groups to finalize and *present* their "Weather Chart Creation" project. Each group creates a large weather chart for "How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?" for their imagined city/region, labeling predictions using future simple tense ('It will be sunny'), drawing weather symbols, and including recommendations ('You should bring an umbrella').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Presentation and Peer Mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their Weather Chart to another group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the presentation, one student from the presenting group *explains* a section of their chart (e.g., "For the morning, it will be cloudy with a chance of rain. The temperature will be cool, around 20°C."). They must *point to their diagrams* on the chart to aid comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student from the *receiving* group acts as a "mediator." After listening, they *confirm understanding* by rephrasing or asking clarification questions (e.g., "So, it will be cool and rainy in the morning, right?" or "Does 'cool' mean warm or cold?"). They might also *summarize* for a third peer, writing keywords on a mini-whiteboard and showing a drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenting group members must *confirm* that the mediator's summary/understanding is correct or *re-explain* using different words and visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then switch roles, ensuring everyone practices mediating and explaining.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills during the group presentations and discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students, in their pairs/groups, demonstrate their ability to explain a weather prediction from their chart using visuals and confirm peer comprehension. They will act as both explainer and mediator for different parts of their "Weather Chart Creation" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of diagrams/visuals (e.g., sun, cloud, rain) to explain weather concepts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Confirmation of peer comprehension (e.g., "Do you understand?", "Is that clear?", asking questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarization of weather-related concepts using keywords and/or simple diagrams.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of future simple tense (e.g., 'It will be sunny').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., `forecast`, `temperature`, `predict`, `rainy`, `cloudy`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation and intonation in predictions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I use drawings to help my friends understand the weather forecast?" (Circle: Very well / Okay / Need more practice)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned about explaining weather to others?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when trying to explain your weather chart to your classmates?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures or keywords help you understand your friends' weather predictions?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The Weather Chart Creation project worked well to integrate all skills and specifically provided a strong context for mediation. Students were engaged in using visuals and keywords to clarify predictions. Some students struggled with consistently asking "Do you understand?" to confirm comprehension; more explicit modeling and cue cards might be needed in future mediation tasks. The focus on intonation was integrated naturally as students presented their forecasts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?" by having students consolidate their learning through mediation. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, building on these communication and collaborative skills, potentially requiring students to mediate information from a new text or audio source.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>